WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:06.540
Speaker 1: I want to start today with some&nbsp;
genuinely good economic news, and the news itself&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:06.540 --> 00:00:12.120
is a very small data point. But there's a bigger&nbsp;
story here, and it's the story first and foremost&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:12.120 --> 00:00:19.140
about how is the economy doing right now. That&nbsp;
gets us to what will the impact of the economy be&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:19.140 --> 00:00:25.440
on the midterms. If people accurately understand&nbsp;
the state of the economy because it's about the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:25.440 --> 00:00:30.780
perception of the economy more than the economy&nbsp;
itself, that affects how people vote. And then&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:30.780 --> 00:00:36.660
the bigger conversation, which I think continues&nbsp;
to be a very interesting one, is what in general&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:36.660 --> 00:00:42.540
can we say about the state of the American economy&nbsp;
when there's a Democrat in the White House versus&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:42.540 --> 00:00:46.980
when there is a Republican in the White House? So&nbsp;
come along for the journey. Let's you know, rather&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:46.980 --> 00:00:54.060
than starting at 30,000 feet and getting down to&nbsp;
the runway. Excuse the analogy. Let's start at the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:54.060 --> 00:01:00.600
base and then get up to 30,000 feet. So economic&nbsp;
news, good news, GDP has accelerated in the United&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:00.600 --> 00:01:06.600
States at 2.6% in the third quarter. We just&nbsp;
got these numbers. Remember, the third quarter&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:07.620 --> 00:01:13.620
is July, August, September. And we get those&nbsp;
numbers at the end of the following month, which&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:13.620 --> 00:01:22.920
is now the end of October. Expectation for GDP&nbsp;
growth was 2.3%. The actual number was 2.6%. So&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:23.520 --> 00:01:29.280
having an increase rather than a decrease by&nbsp;
standard economic metrics where we want indefinite&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:29.280 --> 00:01:35.340
growth, I know it's good to have GDP growth and&nbsp;
then when it is better than the expectation,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:35.340 --> 00:01:40.080
then it is considered even better. And indeed,&nbsp;
the stock market is reacting to this quite&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:40.080 --> 00:01:46.080
favorably as of this particular moment when we&nbsp;
are filming, the Dow Jones Industrial Average&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:46.080 --> 00:01:54.360
is up over 500 points and we will see where the&nbsp;
day finishes out. A narrowing trade deficit and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:54.360 --> 00:02:00.420
increases in consumer spending and government&nbsp;
outlays boosted the number all normal. A sharp&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:00.420 --> 00:02:05.940
pullback in housing subtracted from the number&nbsp;
part of a broader decline in private investment.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:05.940 --> 00:02:12.480
Consumer spending decelerated, increasing at&nbsp;
just a 1.4% pace in the quarter, down from 2%.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:12.480 --> 00:02:18.960
So understand that oftentimes the reports on&nbsp;
this stuff are so unclear, consumer spending&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:18.960 --> 00:02:25.500
decelerated. Might make some think that consumer&nbsp;
spending is down. It is not. It grew at a slightly&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:25.500 --> 00:02:32.100
lower rate than in the previous quarter. So the&nbsp;
obvious question when we get such numbers is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:32.100 --> 00:02:37.980
this must be terrible for Democrats, right? How&nbsp;
will Democrats and Joe Biden ever survive this?&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:37.980 --> 00:02:44.220
Obviously good news. That's a joke. One of my&nbsp;
followers on Twitter said something like, The way&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:44.220 --> 00:02:52.440
Fox News will report it is, is the economy doing&nbsp;
better right now because people expect Republicans&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:52.440 --> 00:02:59.460
to take over in November. So they are spending&nbsp;
now because they know the economy will be better&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:59.460 --> 00:03:06.060
once republic. It's a joke, but it wouldn't be&nbsp;
completely foreign to Fox News to pull something&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:06.060 --> 00:03:11.640
like that. So that's the GDP number nets.&nbsp;
Let's now take a look at the broader economy.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:11.640 --> 00:03:19.680
We have an economy where we have better than&nbsp;
expected GDP growth. Historically low unemployment&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:19.680 --> 00:03:27.180
to the point where one could argue we are within&nbsp;
a few tenths of a point of what we would consider&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:27.180 --> 00:03:34.080
full employment. Remember, full employment isn't&nbsp;
every single person has a job, but by the economic&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:34.080 --> 00:03:41.100
definition of full employment, we're very close to&nbsp;
that. Consumer demand continues to grow, albeit at&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:41.100 --> 00:03:46.740
a slightly slower rate than it did in the second&nbsp;
quarter. And there is still high inflation. Of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:46.740 --> 00:03:50.880
course, there's no there's no need to exclude&nbsp;
that from our analysis or to pretend that that's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:50.880 --> 00:03:57.840
not going on. So it's becoming very difficult to&nbsp;
make the case that this is a terrible economy, but&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:57.840 --> 00:04:02.820
Republicans are still doing it, and that's what&nbsp;
gets us to the impact on the November election.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:03.780 --> 00:04:12.000
There was a point. Eight weeks ago, ten weeks ago,&nbsp;
something like that, where the number of Americans&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:12.000 --> 00:04:20.340
who said abortion access and the overturning of&nbsp;
Roe v Wade are top issues in terms of the midterms&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:21.060 --> 00:04:27.960
that has diminished and has been supplanted&nbsp;
by the economy, which at the end of the day,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:27.960 --> 00:04:35.100
in most elections, it's the economy that&nbsp;
regularly rates as the most important issue. Now,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:35.100 --> 00:04:41.580
sensible people, like many of you would say, well,&nbsp;
David, the the economy's doing well. Most economic&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:41.580 --> 00:04:47.520
indicators are very good. Inflation's high, but&nbsp;
everything else looks great. Clearly, on the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:47.520 --> 00:04:52.740
strength of the economy, Democrats are going to do&nbsp;
really well in November. Right. Not necessarily.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:52.740 --> 00:05:00.660
What matters is the perception of the economy. And&nbsp;
by straight up telling lies and focusing only on&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:00.660 --> 00:05:07.680
inflation. Republicans have convinced many voters&nbsp;
that we have a bad economy right now. Nearly full&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:07.680 --> 00:05:16.140
employment. GDP growth. Choppy, but not crazy.&nbsp;
Stock market. But many voters believe that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:16.140 --> 00:05:21.060
there's something very wrong with the economy.&nbsp;
And if indeed they do believe that, it wouldn't&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:21.060 --> 00:05:26.820
be unexpected for them to vote for Republicans&nbsp;
since they are the out party currently in terms of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:26.820 --> 00:05:32.580
power in the White House, House and Senate. This&nbsp;
then gets us to the last thing I want to discuss&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:32.580 --> 00:05:38.640
a little bit, which is, in general, does the&nbsp;
economy do better under Democratic or Republican&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:38.640 --> 00:05:46.020
presidents? The data are extraordinarily clear&nbsp;
on this. Don't trust me. Do your own research on&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:46.020 --> 00:05:54.300
this. But if you look historically in the modern&nbsp;
political era, on average, the stock market does&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:54.300 --> 00:06:00.780
better under Democratic presidents. Job growth&nbsp;
is stronger under Democratic presidents. The&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:00.780 --> 00:06:05.640
unemployment rate is lower. Under Democratic&nbsp;
presidents, GDP growth is greater under&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:05.640 --> 00:06:11.700
Democratic presidents. Inflation is lower under&nbsp;
Democratic presidents. Are there exceptions? Yes.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:13.380 --> 00:06:19.800
But that is an undeniable reality. Now you will&nbsp;
often hear talking points from the right when&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:19.800 --> 00:06:25.500
confronted with this undeniable data, where they&nbsp;
will say things like, Well, that's because a lot&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:25.500 --> 00:06:31.560
of Democratic presidents had the benefit of a&nbsp;
Republican controlled House to rein them in.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:31.560 --> 00:06:36.840
Such statements have been evaluated empirically,&nbsp;
and they are without merit. Sometimes you will&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:36.840 --> 00:06:43.380
hear, well, there is a lag time. So really&nbsp;
like the first six months of a Democratic&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:43.380 --> 00:06:49.620
administration are the economic result of the&nbsp;
previous administration, which might have been&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:49.620 --> 00:06:57.300
a Republican. It's been studied. And there is no&nbsp;
empirical evidence suggesting that. So don't take&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:57.300 --> 00:07:04.080
my word for it. Unlike many others, I don't tell&nbsp;
you that I hold the ultimate and unique truth or a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:04.080 --> 00:07:10.020
monopoly on the data, or a monopoly on evaluating&nbsp;
the data. Do the research yourself. I've done&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:10.020 --> 00:07:13.380
it myself. I've made the spreadsheets&nbsp;
myself, and I've looked at it closely.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:13.980 --> 00:07:21.300
And unless I have lost every ability to interpret&nbsp;
even the most basic data, this is the reality&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:21.300 --> 00:07:24.480
that I've presented to you. Fact Check me.&nbsp;
Tell me if you find something different.

